## 2. GRAPHQL VS REST — DEEP COMPARISON

### Side-by-Side

| Aspect | REST | GraphQL |
|--------|------|---------|
| **Endpoints** | Multiple URLs (`/users`, `/posts`, `/comments`) | Single URL (`/graphql`) |
| **Data fetching** | Server decides what data to return | Client decides what data to get |
| **Over-fetching** | Returns all fields every time | Returns only requested fields |
| **Under-fetching** | Need multiple requests for related data | Get all related data in one request |
| **Versioning** | `/api/v1/users`, `/api/v2/users` | No versioning needed — evolve schema |
| **Type system** | No built-in types (OpenAPI is optional) | Built-in strong type system (schema) |
| **Documentation** | Must maintain separately (Swagger) | Self-documenting via introspection |
| **Status codes** | Uses HTTP status codes (200, 404, 500) | Always returns 200 (errors in response body) |
| **Caching** | HTTP caching (GET requests) | More complex (needs client libs) |
| **File upload** | Native support | Requires extra spec (multipart) |
| **Learning curve** | Lower | Higher |
| **Tooling** | Postman, curl | GraphiQL, Apollo Studio, Playground |

### Over-fetching Problem (REST)
```
REST: GET /api/users/1
Response: {
  "id": 1,
  "name": "John",
  "email": "john@example.com",
  "phone": "+1234567890",     ← Don't need this
  "address": "123 Main St",   ← Don't need this
  "company": "Acme Corp",     ← Don't need this
  "bio": "Long text...",      ← Don't need this
  "avatar": "url...",         ← Don't need this
  "createdAt": "...",         ← Don't need this
  "updatedAt": "..."          ← Don't need this
}
// Client only needed name and email — got everything else too!
```

```
GraphQL: POST /graphql
Query: {
  user(id: 1) {
    name
    email
  }
}
Response: {
  "data": {
    "user": {
      "name": "John",
      "email": "john@example.com"
    }
  }
}
// Client got exactly what it asked for!
```

### Under-fetching Problem (REST)
```
To display a user profile with their posts and comments:

REST (3 separate requests):
  1. GET /api/users/1            → User data
  2. GET /api/users/1/posts      → User's posts
  3. GET /api/posts/5/comments   → Post comments
  Total: 3 round trips to server

GraphQL (1 request):
  query {
    user(id: 1) {
      name
      email
      posts {
        title
        comments {
          text
          author { name }
        }
      }
    }
  }
  Total: 1 round trip to server
```

### When to Use REST vs GraphQL

| Use REST When | Use GraphQL When |
|---------------|-----------------|
| Simple CRUD APIs | Complex data relationships |
| File serving | Mobile apps (bandwidth matters) |
| Public APIs with caching needs | Multiple client types (web, mobile, TV) |
| Team is already experienced with REST | Flexible data requirements |
| Simple microservices communication | Aggregating data from multiple sources |
| Webhook integrations | Real-time features needed |

