## 5. SCALAR TYPES

### Built-in Scalar Types

| Type | Description | Example |
|------|-------------|---------|
| `Int` | 32-bit signed integer | `42`, `-7` |
| `Float` | Double-precision floating point | `3.14`, `-2.5` |
| `String` | UTF-8 text | `"Hello World"` |
| `Boolean` | `true` or `false` | `true` |
| `ID` | Unique identifier (serialized as String) | `"abc123"`, `"1"` |

### Nullability Rules

```graphql
type Example {
  name: String       # Nullable — can return null
  name: String!      # Non-null — MUST return a value (never null)
  tags: [String]     # Nullable list of nullable strings (null, [], ["a", null])
  tags: [String!]    # Nullable list of non-null strings (null, [], ["a", "b"])
  tags: [String]!    # Non-null list of nullable strings ([], ["a", null])
  tags: [String!]!   # Non-null list of non-null strings ([], ["a", "b"])
}
```

**Cheat Sheet:**
```
String        → null OR "value"
String!       → MUST be "value" (never null)
[String]      → null OR [] OR ["a", null, "b"]
[String!]     → null OR [] OR ["a", "b"]  (items can't be null)
[String]!     → [] OR ["a", null, "b"]    (list can't be null)
[String!]!    → [] OR ["a", "b"]          (nothing can be null)
```

