## 21. DATALOADER — SOLVING THE N+1 PROBLEM

### The N+1 Problem
```graphql
# This query causes N+1 database calls:
query {
  posts {          # 1 query to get 100 posts
    title
    author {       # 100 queries to get each post's author (N queries)
      name
    }
  }
}
# Total: 101 database queries for 100 posts! 😱
```

### Solution: DataLoader
DataLoader **batches** multiple individual loads into a single batch query.

```javascript
import DataLoader from 'dataloader';

// Batch function — receives array of IDs, returns array of results (same order)
async function batchUsers(userIds) {
  // ONE database query instead of N queries
  const users = await db.users.find({ id: { $in: userIds } });
  
  // Must return results in the SAME ORDER as the input IDs
  const userMap = new Map(users.map(u => [u.id.toString(), u]));
  return userIds.map(id => userMap.get(id.toString()) || null);
}

// Create DataLoader in context (one per request)
const context = {
  loaders: {
    userLoader: new DataLoader(batchUsers),
    postLoader: new DataLoader(batchPosts),
    commentLoader: new DataLoader(batchComments),
  },
};

// Use in resolvers
const resolvers = {
  Post: {
    // Before DataLoader: N queries
    // author: (post) => db.users.findById(post.authorId),
    
    // After DataLoader: 1 batched query for all authors
    author: (post, _, context) => context.loaders.userLoader.load(post.authorId),
  },
  
  User: {
    posts: (user, _, context) => context.loaders.postLoader.load(user.id),
  },
};
```

### How DataLoader Works
```
Without DataLoader:
  Post 1 → findUser(1)  → DB query
  Post 2 → findUser(2)  → DB query
  Post 3 → findUser(1)  → DB query (duplicate!)
  Post 4 → findUser(3)  → DB query
  = 4 separate queries

With DataLoader:
  Post 1 → userLoader.load(1)  → queued
  Post 2 → userLoader.load(2)  → queued
  Post 3 → userLoader.load(1)  → cached (deduped!)
  Post 4 → userLoader.load(3)  → queued
  → Next tick: batchUsers([1, 2, 3]) → 1 query!
  = 1 batched query + automatic caching
```

