## 25. CACHING STRATEGIES

### Client-Side Caching (Apollo Client)

Apollo Client uses a **normalized cache** — it stores data by `__typename:id`.

```javascript
import { ApolloClient, InMemoryCache } from '@apollo/client';

const client = new ApolloClient({
  uri: '/graphql',
  cache: new InMemoryCache({
    typePolicies: {
      Query: {
        fields: {
          // Merge paginated results
          posts: {
            keyArgs: ['filter'],
            merge(existing = { edges: [] }, incoming) {
              return {
                ...incoming,
                edges: [...existing.edges, ...incoming.edges],
              };
            },
          },
        },
      },
      User: {
        keyFields: ['id'],  // How to identify unique users
      },
      Post: {
        keyFields: ['id'],
      },
    },
  }),
});
```

### Fetch Policies
```javascript
const { data } = useQuery(GET_USERS, {
  fetchPolicy: 'cache-first',       // Default — check cache first
  // fetchPolicy: 'cache-only',      // Only from cache (no network)
  // fetchPolicy: 'network-only',    // Always fetch from network
  // fetchPolicy: 'cache-and-network', // Return cache + refresh from network
  // fetchPolicy: 'no-cache',        // Never cache
});
```

### Server-Side Caching
```javascript
// Apollo Server cache hints
const resolvers = {
  Query: {
    posts: async (_, args, context, info) => {
      // Set cache control
      info.cacheControl.setCacheHint({ maxAge: 300 }); // 5 minutes
      return await context.db.posts.find();
    },
  },
};

// Schema directive approach
type Query {
  posts: [Post!]! @cacheControl(maxAge: 300)
  me: User @cacheControl(maxAge: 0)  # Never cache
}
```

### HTTP-Level Caching
```
GET requests can use HTTP caching:
  - Apollo Server supports automatic persisted queries (APQ)
  - GET /graphql?query={...}&variables={...}
  - CDN can cache GET requests

POST requests cannot use HTTP caching:
  - All mutations use POST
  - Complex queries typically use POST
```

