## 27. SCHEMA DESIGN BEST PRACTICES

### 1. Use Input Types for Mutations
```graphql
# ✗ BAD — too many inline arguments
mutation {
  createUser(name: String!, email: String!, age: Int, role: Role): User!
}

# ✓ GOOD — use input type
input CreateUserInput {
  name: String!
  email: String!
  age: Int
  role: Role = USER
}
mutation {
  createUser(input: CreateUserInput!): User!
}
```

### 2. Return the Modified Object from Mutations
```graphql
# ✗ BAD — returns boolean (client must refetch)
type Mutation {
  updateUser(id: ID!, name: String!): Boolean!
}

# ✓ GOOD — returns updated object (client cache auto-updates)
type Mutation {
  updateUser(id: ID!, input: UpdateUserInput!): User!
}
```

### 3. Use Payload Types for Complex Mutations
```graphql
# ✓ BEST — use payload type
type UpdateUserPayload {
  user: User
  errors: [FieldError!]
}

type FieldError {
  field: String!
  message: String!
}

type Mutation {
  updateUser(id: ID!, input: UpdateUserInput!): UpdateUserPayload!
}
```

### 4. Use Connections for Lists (Relay Style)
```graphql
# ✗ BAD — no pagination info
type Query {
  posts: [Post!]!
}

# ✓ GOOD — with pagination
type Query {
  posts(first: Int, after: String): PostConnection!
}
```

### 5. Prefix Boolean Fields with "is" or "has"
```graphql
type User {
  isActive: Boolean!
  isVerified: Boolean!
  hasAvatar: Boolean!
  canEdit: Boolean!
}
```

### 6. Use Enums for Fixed Values
```graphql
# ✗ BAD — string
type User {
  role: String!    # Can be anything: "admn", "ADMIN", "admin"
}

# ✓ GOOD — enum
enum Role { ADMIN USER MODERATOR }
type User {
  role: Role!      # Type-safe, auto-documented
}
```

### 7. Keep the Schema Flat (Avoid Deep Nesting in Types)
```graphql
# ✗ BAD — excessive depth in types
type Query {
  company: Company
}
type Company {
  departments: [Department]
}
type Department {
  teams: [Team]
}
type Team {
  members: [User]  # 4 levels deep!
}

# ✓ GOOD — provide direct access points
type Query {
  company(id: ID!): Company
  department(id: ID!): Department
  team(id: ID!): Team
  users(teamId: ID): [User!]!
}
```

### 8. Use @deprecated Instead of Removing Fields
```graphql
type User {
  name: String!
  username: String @deprecated(reason: "Use 'name' field instead. Will be removed on 2025-01-01")
}
```

